== Root Systems and Finite Reflection Groups
<sec:root-systems-reflection-groups>

=== Finite Groups Generated by Reflections. Root Systems
<ss:finite-reflection-root-systems>

Throughout this section, let $V$ denote a f.d. vector space over the real field
$RR$, and assume $V$ is equipped with a symmetric, positive definite bilinear
form taking values $(xi,eta) in RR$, for $xi,eta in V$. We call $V$ a real
_euclidean space_, and refer to the bilinear form $(xi,eta)$ as an _inner
product_ on $V$. The form is of course nondegenerate. The _group of orthogonal
transformations_ on $V$ is defined by
$ O(V)={s in End_RR V:(s xi,s eta)=(xi,eta) "for all" xi,eta in V}. $

#source(567)
For a subspace $U$ of $V$, we set
$ U^perp={xi in V:(xi,eta)=0 "for all" eta in U}, $
and call $U^perp$ the _orthogonal complement_ of $U$.

For a set of elements ${s_i}$ of $O(V)$, let $chevron.l s_1,s_2,... chevron.r$
denote the subgroup of $O(V)$ that they generate. We shall also use the notation
$chevron.l alpha,beta,... chevron.r$ for the subspace of $V$ generated by the
vectors $alpha,beta,...$ of $V$.

#definition[
  A _reflection_ is a linear map $s:V arrow.r V$ such that $s in O(V)$, $s!=1$,
  and $s$ fixes every vector in some hyperplane in $V$.
] <def:euclidean-reflection>
#idx("Reflection", target: [@def:euclidean-reflection])

#proposition[
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [#case[
      Let $H$ be a hyperplane in $V$. There exists a unique reflection
      $s in O(V)$ that leaves fixed the elements of $H$. Then $s^2=1$, and $s$
      is specified by the formula
      $
        s xi=xi-frac(2(xi,alpha), (alpha,alpha)) alpha
        quad "for all" xi in V,
      $
      where $alpha$ is an arbitrary nonzero vector in $H^perp$. Note that
      $s alpha=-alpha$.
    ] <case:reflection-hyperplane-formula>],
    [#case[
      Let $alpha$ be a given nonzero vector in $V$, and let $s_alpha$ be the
      reflection in the hyperplane $H=chevron.l alpha chevron.r^perp$. Then for
      all $g in O(V)$,
      $ g s_alpha g^(-1)=s_(g alpha). $
    ] <case:orthogonal-conjugate-reflection>],
  )
] <prop:reflection-formula-conjugacy>

#proof[
  @case:reflection-hyperplane-formula Let $H^perp=chevron.l alpha chevron.r$, so
  $H=chevron.l alpha chevron.r^perp$, and let $s$ be any reflection fixing each
  element of $H$. Then $s alpha in H^perp$ since $s in O(V)$, and it follows
  that $s alpha=-alpha$, using the fact that $s!=1$. From the equality
  $ V=H plus.o chevron.l alpha chevron.r, $
  we have $s^2=1$. Now let $xi in V$ be expressed as
  $ xi=eta+t alpha, quad eta in H, quad t in RR. $
  Then $t=lr((xi,alpha))/lr((alpha,alpha))$, and
  $ s xi=eta-t alpha=xi-2t alpha. $
  This proves the formula for $s$, and establishes the uniqueness of $s$. It is
  also clear that the formula for $s$ does indeed define a reflection fixing the
  elements of the hyperplane $chevron.l alpha chevron.r^perp$.

  @case:orthogonal-conjugate-reflection Clearly $g s_alpha g^(-1) in O(V)$ has
  order 2, and fixes the elements of the hyperplane
  $chevron.l g alpha chevron.r^perp$. Therefore $g s_alpha g^(-1)=s_(g alpha)$
  by part~@case:reflection-hyperplane-formula.
]

#source(568)
#definition[
  A _group generated by reflections_ (abbreviated as g.g.r.) is a subgroup of
  $O(V)$ generated by reflections.
] <def:group-generated-reflections>
#nidx(
  "g.g.r.",
  "group generated by reflections",
  target: [@def:group-generated-reflections],
)
#idx(
  "Group generated by reflections (g.g.r.)",
  target: [@def:group-generated-reflections],
)

We shall be mainly interested in finite g.g.r.'s. Their structure and
classification will be obtained by using combinatorial properties of finite sets
of vectors in $V$, called root systems, which are permuted by the elements of
the g.g.r. (These root systems first arose in the classification of semisimple
Lie algebras over $CC$; see Bourbaki @bib:Bourbaki1968 for a historical
account.) Our approach follows, to some extent, Steinberg @bib:Steinberg1967 and
Chevalley @bib:Chevalley1956. From the point of view of abstract group theory,
we are studying certain finite groups generated by involutions.#footnote[
  Recall that an _involution_ in a group is an element of order 2.
] <fn:involution-order-two>

#definitions[
  A _root system_ $Delta$ in $V$ is a finite set of vectors ${alpha,beta,...}$
  satisfying the conditions
  @case:root-system-spanning–@case:root-system-reflection-invariance below.
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [#case[$0 in.not Delta$, and the vectors in $Delta$ span $V$.]
      <case:root-system-spanning>],
    [#case[
      (_Reduced condition_) If $alpha in Delta$, then $-alpha in Delta$;
      further, if $c in RR$ is such that $c alpha in Delta$, then
      $c=plus.minus 1$.
    ] <case:root-system-reduced>],
    [#case[
      For each $alpha in Delta$, we have $s_alpha Delta=Delta$, where $s_alpha$
      is the reflection fixing the hyperplane $chevron.l alpha chevron.r^perp$.
    ] <case:root-system-reflection-invariance>],
  )
  A _crystallographic root system_ is a root system $Delta$ satisfying the
  additional condition:
  #case-list(numbering: "(i)", start: 4, [#case[
    (_Crystallographic condition_) For all pairs of roots $alpha,beta in Delta$,
    we have $2lr((alpha,beta))/lr((beta,beta)) in ZZ$.
  ] <case:root-system-crystallographic>])
  The g.g.r. $W=W(Delta)$ generated by the reflections
  ${s_alpha:alpha in Delta}$ is called the g.g.r. associated with the root
  system $Delta$.
] <def:root-system>
#idx("Root system", target: [@def:root-system])
#idx("Crystallographic root system", target: [@def:root-system])

#remarks(numbered: false)[
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [#case[
      It can be proved (see @rem:crystallographic-integral-simple-roots) that a
      crystallographic root system can be embedded in a full $ZZ$-lattice in
      $V$. The g.g.r.'s associated with crystallographic root systems are
      sometimes called _Weyl groups_, and can be identified with the Weyl groups
      of semisimple Lie algebras over $CC$ with respect to Cartan subalgebras
      (see Humphreys @bib:Humphreys1972).
    ] <case:crystallographic-weyl-groups>],
    [#case[
      Let $Delta$ be a root system in $V$. Since $W$ permutes the elements of
      $Delta$, and $Delta$ contains a basis of $V$, it follows that the g.g.r.
      $W(Delta)$ is a finite group.

      Conversely, let $W$ be a finite g.g.r., and let $Delta$ be the set of unit
      vectors orthogonal to the hyperplanes fixed by reflections in $W$. By
      @prop:reflection-formula-conjugacy@case:orthogonal-conjugate-reflection,
      it follows that $W$ permutes the elements of $Delta$. Letting
      $chevron.l Delta chevron.r$ denote the subspace of $V$ generated by
      $Delta$, we have
      $V=chevron.l Delta chevron.r plus.o chevron.l Delta chevron.r^perp$. The
      elements of $W$ leave the subspaces $chevron.l Delta chevron.r$ and
      $chevron.l Delta chevron.r^perp$ invariant, and act trivially on
      $chevron.l Delta chevron.r^perp$; hence $W$ acts faithfully on
      $chevron.l Delta chevron.r$. It is then clear that $Delta$ is a root
      system in $chevron.l Delta chevron.r$ whose Weyl group is isomorphic to
      $W$; thus every finite g.g.r. is associated with a root system.
    ] <case:finite-reflection-root-system-correspondence>],
  )
] <rem:root-system-reflection-group-correspondence>
#idx(
  "Weyl group of a root system",
  target: [@rem:root-system-reflection-group-correspondence],
)

#examples(numbered: false)[
  We shall consider some examples of root systems in vector spaces $V$ #source(
    569,
  ) of dimensions 1 or 2, with the standard euclidean inner product. These
  examples include cases where not all the roots have the same length.

  If $dim V=1$, the only possibility for a root system is a pair of opposing
  vectors ${plus.minus alpha}$, associated with the g.g.r.
  $chevron.l s_alpha chevron.r$ of order 2.

  Root systems in the plane are associated with dihedral groups. An arbitrary
  finite dihedral group $D_m$ of order $2m$ is isomorphic to the symmetry group
  of a regular $m$-sided polygon in the plane. It is generated by a rotation $r$
  of order $m$ and a reflection $s$, so that
  $ D_m=chevron.l r,s:r^m=1,s^2=1,s r s^(-1)=r^(-1) chevron.r. $
  There are exactly $m$ reflections in $D_m$, and $D_m$ is generated by the
  reflections it contains. The unit vectors orthogonal to the hyperplanes fixed
  by the reflections in $D_m$ form a root system in the plane whose g.g.r. is
  $D_m$, by the preceding remark.

  The two-dimensional crystallographic root systems are given as follows. The
  #rank-two-roots()
  notations $A_1 times A_1$, $A_2$, etc. are taken from Cartan's classification
  of crystallographic root systems. The g.g.r.'s associated with these root
  systems are dihedral, of orders 4, 6, 8, and 12, respectively. This
  observation, together with the preceding paragraph, shows that the dihedral
  groups of orders 8 and 12 are each associated with two root systems, one of
  which is crystallographic, and one of which is not. The point is that to
  satisfy the crystallographic condition, the lengths of the roots cannot be
  chosen arbitrarily.

  Here is a sketch of the proof that the root systems of type $A_1 times A_1$,
  $A_2$, $B_2$, and $G_2$ are the only crystallographic root systems in two
  dimensions. Let $Delta$ be a crystallographic root system, and let
  $alpha,beta$ be linearly independent roots in $Delta$. If $theta$ denotes the
  angle between $alpha$ and $beta$, then
  $
    frac(4(alpha,beta)(beta,alpha), (alpha,alpha)(beta,beta))
    =4 cos^2 theta<4.
  $
  The crystallographic condition then implies that
  $
    2lr((alpha,beta))/lr((beta,beta))
    in {0,plus.minus 1,plus.minus 2,plus.minus 3},
  $
  from which one readily obtains the above classification.
] <exm:rank-two-root-systems>
#idx("Dihedral group", target: [@exm:rank-two-root-systems])

#source(570)
In case $V$ has dimension $n$, the symmetric groups $S_(n+1)$ and the
hyperoctahedral groups $H_n$ are g.g.r.'s associated with crystallographic root
systems in $V$ (see Exercises~@exc:type-a-root-system,
@exc:hyperoctahedral-root-systems).

Returning to the general discussion, we shall begin to study an arbitrary finite
g.g.r. $W$ associated with a root system $Delta$ in $V$, in terms of order
relations on $V$ and $Delta$. Every nonzero vector $xi in V$ defines a partition
of $V$:
$ V=V_(-) lr((xi)) union H_xi union V_(+) lr((xi)), $
where $H_xi$ is the hyperplane $chevron.l xi chevron.r^perp$, and
$
  V_(+) lr((xi))={lambda in V:(lambda,xi)>0}, quad
  V_(-) lr((xi))={lambda in V:(lambda,xi)<0}.
$
Since $Delta$ is a finite set, there exist vectors $xi$ such that
$(xi,alpha)!=0$ for all $alpha in Delta$. Let us fix such an element $xi$. We
then have a partition of $Delta$ into positive roots $Delta_+$ and negative
roots $Delta_-$, defined by
$
  Delta_+=Delta inter V_(+) lr((xi)), quad
  Delta_-=Delta inter V_(-) lr((xi)).
$
We have:

#lemma[
  Let $xi in V$ be such that $(xi,alpha)!=0$ for each $alpha in Delta$. Suppose
  that $sum c_alpha alpha=0$ for some set of positive roots $alpha in Delta_+$,
  with nonnegative real coefficients ${c_alpha}$. Then each $c_alpha=0$.
] <lem:positive-root-cone-pointed>

#proof[
  From $sum c_alpha alpha=0$, we obtain $sum c_alpha (alpha,xi)=0$. Since
  $(alpha,xi)>0$ for each $alpha$, it follows that each $c_alpha=0$.
]

Given a finite set of vectors ${alpha_i}$, by their _positive span_ we mean the
collection of all vectors of the form $sum c_i alpha_i$ with coefficients
$c_i>=0$, and not all $c_i=0$. For example, $Delta_+$ is precisely the set of
roots in $Delta$ that belong to the positive span of $Delta_+$. We now
establish:

#theorem[
  Let $Pi$ be a subset of $Delta_+$ such that $Delta_+$ is the set of all roots
  contained in the positive span of $Pi$, and let $Pi$ be minimal with this
  property. Then the following statements hold:
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [#case[$Pi$ is a basis of $V$.] <case:fundamental-root-basis>],
    [#case[
      Every root in $Delta$ can be expressed in the form
      $plus.minus (sum a_i alpha_i)$, with $alpha_i in Pi$, and nonnegative real
      coefficients ${a_i}$.
    ] <case:root-simple-coefficients-common-sign>],
    [#case[
      For all pairs of distinct roots $alpha_i,alpha_j$ in $Pi$ we have
      $ (alpha_i,alpha_j)<=0. $
    ] <case:fundamental-roots-obtuse>],
  )
] <th:fundamental-root-basis>

#proof[
  We begin with the proof of @case:root-simple-coefficients-common-sign. As we
  observed in the remark preceding #source(571) the statement, $Delta_+$ is the
  set of roots in the positive span of $Delta_+$, so that minimal subsets
  $Pi subset.eq Delta_+$ having this property do exist. For such a subset $Pi$,
  @case:root-simple-coefficients-common-sign clearly holds, by the definition of
  positive span, since $Delta=Delta_+ union Delta_-$.

  We next prove @case:fundamental-roots-obtuse, and will use
  @case:fundamental-roots-obtuse to prove that $Pi$ is a linearly independent
  set. Let
  $
    Pi={alpha_1,...,alpha_n}, quad
    a_(i j)=2lr((alpha_i,alpha_j))/lr((alpha_j,alpha_j)), quad s_i=s_(alpha_i),
  $
  for $alpha_i in Pi$. Then, for all $i$ and $j$,
  $ s_i lr((alpha_j))=alpha_j-a_(j i) alpha_i, $
  and either $s_i alpha_j in Delta_+$ or $-s_i alpha_j in Delta_+$. Thus one of
  $alpha_j-a_(j i) alpha_i$, $-alpha_j+a_(j i) alpha_i$ lies in $Delta_+$. Then
  @case:fundamental-roots-obtuse will follow from:
]

#lemma[
  For all pairs of distinct roots $alpha_i,alpha_j in Pi$, it is impossible to
  have $c_i alpha_i-c_j alpha_j in Delta_+$, with both $c_i,c_j>0$.
] <lem:minimal-positive-roots-no-positive-difference>

#proof(head: emph[Proof of Lemma.])[
  Suppose, to the contrary, that $c_i alpha_i-c_j alpha_j in Delta_+$ with
  $c_i,c_j>0$. Then
  $ c_i alpha_i-c_j alpha_j=sum_k a_k alpha_k, $
  where $a_k>=0$ for each $k$. If $c_i<=a_i$, then
  $ sum_(k!=i,j) a_k alpha_k+(a_i-c_i) alpha_i+(a_j+c_j) alpha_j=0, $
  with all coefficients nonnegative, and $a_j+c_j>0$, contrary to
  Lemma~@lem:positive-root-cone-pointed. On the other hand, if $c_i>a_i$, then
  $ (c_i-a_i) alpha_i=c_j alpha_j+sum_(k!=i) a_k alpha_k, $
  which allows us to express $alpha_i$ as a positive linear combination of other
  roots in $Pi$, contrary to the minimality of $Pi$. This completes the proof of
  Lemma~@lem:minimal-positive-roots-no-positive-difference, and
  part~@case:fundamental-roots-obtuse of the theorem.
]

In order to prove @case:fundamental-root-basis, it is sufficient to show that
$Pi$ is a linearly independent set, since we already know that $Delta_+$ spans
$V$, and hence so does $Pi$. Using part~@case:fundamental-roots-obtuse, it is
enough to prove:

#lemma[
  Let ${alpha_1,...,alpha_m}$ be a set of positive roots such that
  $(alpha_i,alpha_j)<=0$ for all $i!=j$. Then ${alpha_1,...,alpha_m}$ is a
  linearly independent set.
] <lem:obtuse-positive-roots-independent>

#proof[
  Suppose, to the contrary, that ${alpha_1,...,alpha_m}$ are linearly dependent.
  By #source(572) Lemma~@lem:positive-root-cone-pointed, a relation of linear
  dependence must have the form
  $
    sum_(i in I) a_i alpha_i=sum_(j in J) b_j alpha_j!=0,
    quad "where all" a_i,b_j>0,
  $
  for two nonempty disjoint subsets $I,J$ of the index set ${1,...,m}$. Putting
  $rho=sum a_i alpha_i=sum b_j alpha_j$, we have $(rho,rho)>0$ by the positivity
  of the inner product, while on the other hand
  $ (rho,rho)=sum a_i b_j (alpha_i,alpha_j)<=0. $
  This contradiction proves the lemma, and completes the proof of the theorem.
]

#definition[
  A set of roots $Pi$ satisfying conditions
  @case:fundamental-root-basis–@case:fundamental-roots-obtuse of
  Theorem~@th:fundamental-root-basis is called a _fundamental system_ (or a
  _simple system_), and the roots $alpha_i in Pi$ are called _fundamental roots_
  (or _simple roots_). The reflections ${s_(alpha_i):alpha_i in Pi}$ are called
  _fundamental reflections_ (relative to $Pi$).
] <def:fundamental-root-system>
#idx("Fundamental roots", target: [@def:fundamental-root-system])
#idx("Simple roots", target: [@def:fundamental-root-system])
#idx("Fundamental reflections", target: [@def:fundamental-root-system])

#corollary[
  Every positive set of roots $Delta_+$, as defined above, contains a unique
  fundamental system $Pi$. Conversely, each fundamental system $Pi$ is contained
  in a unique positive set of roots $Delta_+=V_(+) lr((xi)) inter Delta$, for
  some nonzero vector $xi$.
] <cor:positive-roots-unique-fundamental-system>

#proof[
  For the first statement, it follows from the theorem that $Pi$ is
  characterized as the set of those elements of $Delta_+$ that cannot be
  expressed as positive linear combinations of two or more elements of
  $Delta_+$. Conversely, if $Pi$ is a fundamental system, then $Pi$ is a basis
  of $V$, and since $V$ can be identified with its dual space using the inner
  product, there certainly exist nonzero vectors $xi$ in $V$ such that
  $Pi subset V_(+) lr((xi))$. For any such $xi$, it follows from the definition
  of $Pi$ that $Delta_+=V_(+) lr((xi)) inter Delta$ is the set of roots which
  are positive linear combinations of the elements of $Pi$. Thus $Delta_+$ is
  independent of the choice of $xi$, and is unique.
]

#remark(numbered: true)[
  Let $Delta$ be a crystallographic root system, and let $Pi$ be a fundamental
  system. An analysis of the proof of Theorem~@th:fundamental-root-basis shows
  that all the roots in $Delta$ are $ZZ$-linear combinations of the fundamental
  roots, and hence $Delta$ spans a $ZZ$-lattice on which the Weyl group acts.
  (This fact explains the term “crystallographic root system”.)
] <rem:crystallographic-integral-simple-roots>

#definition[
  Let $Pi$ be a fundamental system in an arbitrary root system $Delta$, and let
  $Delta_+$ be the positive system determined by $Pi$ (see
  @cor:positive-roots-unique-fundamental-system). Let $alpha in Delta_+$, and
  define the _height_ of $alpha$ (notation: $op("ht") alpha$), by
  $
    op("ht") alpha=sum a_i quad "if" alpha=sum a_i alpha_i,
    quad alpha_i in Pi.
  $
] <def:positive-root-height>

Note that since all the coefficients $a_i$ are nonnegative, $op("ht") alpha>0$
for each $alpha in Delta_+$.

The next result is the key to the structure of the Weyl group $W$ of the root
system $Delta$.

#source(573)
#lemma(name: "Main Lemma")[
  Let $Pi$ be a fundamental system and $Delta_+$ the positive system containing
  $Pi$. Let $alpha in Pi$, and let $beta in Delta_+$ be a positive root such
  that $beta!=alpha$. Then $s_alpha beta in Delta_+$. Thus each fundamental
  reflection $s_alpha$, $alpha in Pi$, permutes the positive roots different
  from $alpha$.
] <lem:simple-reflection-positive-roots>

#proof[
  Let $Pi={alpha,alpha_2,...,alpha_n}$; then $beta in Delta_+$ can be expressed
  as $beta=c_1 alpha+sum_(j=2)^n c_j alpha_j$, with each $c_j>=0$, $1<=j<=n$.
  Since $beta!=alpha$, the reduced property
  @def:root-system@case:root-system-reduced of $Delta$ implies that at least one
  of $c_2,...,c_n$ is nonzero, say $c_2$. Then $s_alpha beta=beta+c alpha$ for
  some $c in RR$, so the coefficient of the fundamental root $alpha_2$ in
  $s_alpha beta$ is $c_2$. Since $c_2>0$, it follows from the definition of a
  fundamental system that $s_alpha beta$ must lie in $Delta_+$, as required.
]

#theorem[
  Let $Pi={alpha_1,...,alpha_n}$ be a fundamental system in $Delta$. Then the
  g.g.r. $W=W(Delta)$ is generated by the fundamental reflections
  ${s_(alpha_i):alpha_i in Pi}$. Moreover, every root $alpha$ is in the
  $W$-orbit of some fundamental root.
] <th:fundamental-reflections-generate>

#proof[
  Let $W_0$ be the subgroup of $W$ generated by the fundamental reflections
  ${s_(alpha_i):alpha_i in Pi}$, and let $Delta_+$ be the positive system
  containing $Pi$, as in @cor:positive-roots-unique-fundamental-system.
  #case-list(
    numbering: "Step 1.",
    [#case[
      Let $alpha in Delta_+$, and suppose that $alpha in.not Pi$. We assert that
      $(alpha,alpha_i)>0$ for some $alpha_i in Pi$. Assume, to the contrary,
      that $(alpha,alpha_i)<=0$ for all $alpha_i in Pi$. Then $Pi union {alpha}$
      is a linearly independent set by @lem:obtuse-positive-roots-independent,
      contradicting the fact that $Pi$ is a basis of $V$.
    ] <case:root-height-positive-simple-pairing>],
    [#case[
      We assert that every positive root $alpha in Delta_+$ of minimal height is
      in $Pi$. For if $alpha in.not Pi$, then $(alpha,alpha_i)>0$ for some
      $alpha_i in Pi$, by @case:root-height-positive-simple-pairing. Then
      $s_(alpha_i) alpha in Delta_+$ by the Main Lemma
      @lem:simple-reflection-positive-roots, and $s_(alpha_i) alpha=
      alpha-[2lr((alpha,alpha_i))/lr((alpha_i,alpha_i))]alpha_i$. Since
      $(alpha,alpha_i)>0$, it follows that
      $op("ht") s_(alpha_i) alpha<op("ht") alpha$, which is impossible. This
      completes the proof of this step.
    ] <case:minimal-height-root-simple>],
    [#case[
      We now prove that every positive root $alpha$ is in the $W_0$-orbit of
      some element of $Pi$, by using induction on $op("ht") alpha$. The result
      is true for positive roots of minimal height by
      @case:minimal-height-root-simple. Now let $alpha in Delta_+$,
      $alpha in.not Pi$. By the proof of @case:minimal-height-root-simple, there
      exists $alpha_i in Pi$ such that $s_(alpha_i) alpha in Delta_+$ and
      $op("ht") s_(alpha_i) alpha<op("ht") alpha$, so we can apply induction to
      conclude that $w dot s_(alpha_i) alpha in Pi$ for some $w in W_0$,
      completing the proof of the assertion.
    ] <case:positive-root-simple-orbit>],
    [#case[
      We now prove that every root is in the $W_0$-orbit of some element of
      $Pi$. By @case:positive-root-simple-orbit, it is sufficient to consider a
      root $beta in Delta_-$. Then $-beta in Delta_+$, and $-beta=w alpha_i$ for
      some $w in W_0$ and some $alpha_i in Pi$, by
      @case:positive-root-simple-orbit. We then have
      $beta=w s_(alpha_i) alpha_i$, and $w s_(alpha_i) in W_0$, as required.
    ] <case:all-roots-simple-orbit>],
  )
  At this point we have proved the second statement of the theorem. To prove the
  first, namely that $W_0=W$, it is enough to show that $s_alpha in W_0$ for
  each root $alpha$. By @case:root-height-positive-simple-pairing–
  @case:all-roots-simple-orbit, there exist $alpha_i in Pi$ and $w in W_0$ such
  that $alpha=w dot alpha_i$. Then $s_alpha=w s_(alpha_i) w^(-1)$ by
  @prop:reflection-formula-conjugacy, and $w s_(alpha_i) w^(-1) in W_0$,
  completing the proof of the theorem.
]

Our next objective, to be completed in §~@ss:coxeter-groups, is to obtain a
presentation of an arbitrary finite g.g.r. $W$, using a set of fundamental
reflections ${s_(alpha_i):alpha_i in Pi}$ as #source(574) generators (see
Definition~@i:def:group-presentation). The determination of the relations
satisfied by the reflections ${s_(alpha_i):alpha_i in Pi}$ is based on the
following analysis of the lengths of words in $W$ in terms of properties of
$Delta$. Note, in particular, that for each $n$ the symmetric group $S_n$ is a
finite g.g.r. (see Exercise~@exc:type-a-root-system). Thus, as a special case of
the discussion below, we will obtain a presentation of $S_n$ in terms of
generators and relations. In interpreting some of the preliminary results below,
the reader will find it useful to keep the example of $S_n$ in mind.

#definitions[
  Let $Delta$ be a root system, with a given set $Delta_+$ of positive roots and
  a fundamental system $Pi subset.eq Delta_+$. For each $w in W$, we define its
  _length_ $l(w)$ as the minimal number of factors needed in expressing $w$ as a
  product of fundamental reflections, as in
  Theorem~@th:fundamental-reflections-generate. An expression
  $ w=s_(alpha_1) dots s_(alpha_k), quad alpha_i in Pi, quad 1<=i<=k, $
  is called _reduced_ if $l(w)=k$. For each $w in W$, we also define two subsets
  $Delta_w^+$ and $Delta_w^-$ of $Delta$ by
  $ Delta_w^(plus.minus)={alpha in Delta_+:w alpha in Delta_(plus.minus)}, $
  and finally set
  $ N(w)=abs(Delta_w^-), quad w in W. $
] <def:reflection-length-inversions>
#idx("Length l(w)", target: [@def:reflection-length-inversions])
#idx("Reduced expression", target: [@def:reflection-length-inversions])
#nidx("$l(w)$", "length of w", target: [@def:reflection-length-inversions])

The remarkable fact that $l(w)=N(w)$, along with other facts that are basic in
determining a presentation of $W$, are contained in the next result, the parts
of which are so closely related that it is best to discuss them as a package.
The theme is to relate properties of $W$ as an abstract group to geometric and
combinatorial properties of $Delta$.

First we need a remark on notation. As usual, we let $Pi={alpha_1,...,alpha_n}$
be a fundamental system in $Delta$; let $Delta_+$ be the positive system
containing $Pi$, and let $S={s_1,...,s_n}$ be the set of fundamental
reflections, where $s_i$ is an abbreviation for $s_(alpha_i)$. Each $w in W$ is
expressible as a product of the ${s_i}$, and we shall write
$ w=s(1)s(2) dots s(m), $
where repetitions may occur, and where $s(i)$ is a fundamental reflection
corresponding to the fundamental root $alpha(i) in Pi$. We are now ready to
establish:

#theorem[
  Let $Pi$, $Delta_+$ and $S$ be as above. Then we have:
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [#case[
      For each $w in W$ and each $s_i in S$,
      $ abs(Delta_(w s_i)^-)=abs(Delta_w^-)plus.minus 1, $
      the sign depending on whether $w alpha_i in Delta_+$ or $Delta_-$.
    ] <case:inversion-simple-reflection-change>],
    [#case[
      #source(575) (_Cancellation Law_). Let $w=s(1) dots s(m)$, with
      $s(i) in S$, and suppose that $m>N(w)$. Then there exist integers $i,j$
      (not necessarily distinct), with $1<=i<=j<=m-1$, such that
      #case-list(
        numbering: "(a)",
        [#case[
          if $i<j$, then
          $
            alpha(i)=s(i+1) dots s(j) alpha(j+1) quad "and"
            s(i+1) dots s(j+1)=s(i) dots s(j),
          $
          or
        ] <case:cancellation-distinct-positions>],
        [#case[
          if $i=j$, the above equations are interpreted as
          $ alpha(i)=alpha(i+1) quad "and" s(i+1)=s(i). $
        ] <case:cancellation-adjacent-positions>],
      )
      In either case, we have
      $ w=s(1) dots hat(s)(i) dots hat(s)(j+1) dots s(m), $
      where the notation indicates that the $i$-th and $(j+1)$-st factors have
      been deleted from the expression for $w$.
    ] <case:reflection-word-cancellation>],
    [#case[For each $w in W$, we have $l(w)=N(w)$.]
      <case:reflection-length-inversion-count>],
    [#case[
      (_Exchange Condition_). Suppose that
      $ l(s(1) dots s(m))=m quad "and" l(s(0)s(1) dots s(m))<m+1, $
      where each $s(i) in S$. Then for some $j$, $0<=j<=m-1$, we have
      $ s(0)s(1) dots s(j)=s(1)s(2) dots s(j+1). $
    ] <case:reflection-exchange-condition>],
    [#case[If $w in W$ is such that $w(Delta_+)=Delta_+$, then $w=1$.]
      <case:positive-root-stabilizer-trivial>],
    [#case[
      There exists a unique element $w_0 in W$ of maximal length. This element
      has the properties: $l(w_0)>=l(w)$ for all $w in W$, and
      $ l(w_0)=abs(Delta_+), quad w_0 Delta_+=Delta_-, quad w_0^2=1. $
    ] <case:longest-reflection-group-element>],
    [#case[
      An element $w in W$ has a reduced expression ending with a given
      $s_i in S$ if and only if $w alpha_i<0$, where $s_i$ is the reflection
      associated with the fundamental root $alpha_i$.
    ] <case:reflection-right-descent>],
  )
] <th:reflection-length-cancellation-exchange>
#idx("Cancellation law", target: [@th:reflection-length-cancellation-exchange])
#idx(
  "Exchange condition",
  target: [@th:reflection-length-cancellation-exchange],
)

#proof[
  We shall use the Main Lemma~@lem:simple-reflection-positive-roots constantly.

  @case:inversion-simple-reflection-change First assume that
  $w alpha_i in Delta_+$. We shall prove that
  $Delta_(w s_i)^-={alpha_i} union s_i lr((Delta_w^-))$ (disjoint union), where
  as above
  $ Delta_w^-={alpha in Delta_+:w alpha in Delta_-}. $
  Now $s_i alpha_i=-alpha_i$, so from the hypothesis $w alpha_i in Delta_+$ we
  deduce that $alpha_i in Delta_(w s_i)^-$. Further, #source(576)
  $alpha_i in.not Delta_w^-$, and also $alpha_i in.not s_i lr((Delta_w^-))$,
  since otherwise $-alpha_i=s_i alpha_i in Delta_w^-$ (using $s_i^2=1$), which
  is contrary to the fact that $Delta_w^-$ consists of positive roots. By the
  Main Lemma, $s_i Delta_w^-$ consists of positive roots, and
  $s_i Delta_w^- subset Delta_(w s_i)^-$, so $Delta_(w s_i)^-$ contains the
  disjoint sets ${alpha_i}$ and $s_i Delta_w^-$. Now let
  $beta in Delta_(w s_i)^-$, and suppose $beta!=alpha_i$. Then
  $w s_i beta in Delta_-$, and $s_i beta in Delta_+$ by the Main Lemma, so
  $s_i beta in Delta_w^-$. Then $beta in s_i Delta_w^-$ since $s_i^2=1$, and the
  result is proved.

  On the other hand, if $w alpha_i in Delta_-$, we have
  $w s_i lr((alpha_i)) in Delta_+$, and we can apply the first statement to get
  $ Delta_w^-=Delta_(w s_i s_i)^-={alpha_i} union s_i lr((Delta_(w s_i)^-)). $
  Thus $abs(Delta_w^-)-1=abs(Delta_(w s_i)^-)$, and the result follows.

  @case:reflection-word-cancellation Suppose next that $w=s(1) dots s(m)$ where
  $N(w)<m$. Then by part~@case:inversion-simple-reflection-change we have
  $s(1) dots s(j) alpha(j+1) in Delta_-$ for some $j<=m-1$. Also, since
  $alpha(j+1) in Delta_+$, there exists $i<=j$ such that $alpha(j)=alpha(j+1)$
  if $i=j$, while if $i<j$ then
  $
    alpha(j+1) in Delta_+, quad s(j) alpha(j+1) in Delta_+, quad ...,
    quad s(i+1) dots s(j) alpha(j+1) in Delta_+,
  $
  and
  $ s(i) dots s(j) alpha(j+1) in Delta_-. $
  By the Main Lemma, it follows that, in the case $i<j$,
  $ s(i+1) dots s(j) alpha(j+1)=alpha(i); $
  hence, by
  @prop:reflection-formula-conjugacy@case:orthogonal-conjugate-reflection,
  $ s(i)=s(i+1) dots s(j)s(j+1)s(j) dots s(i+1). $
  Unwinding this expression (using $s(i)^2=1$), we obtain
  $ s(i) dots s(j)=s(i+1) dots s(j+1). $
  The last statement in the cancellation law (whether $i<j$ or not) follows by
  substituting what has been obtained so far in the original expression for $w$,
  again using the fact that $s(k)^2=1$ for each $k$.

  @case:reflection-length-inversion-count Let $w=s(1) dots s(m)$, $s(i) in S$,
  $1<=i<=m$, and assume that $l(w)=m$. By
  part~@case:reflection-word-cancellation we have $N(w)>=m$, since otherwise the
  expression could be shortened. On the other hand, $N(w)<=m$ by
  part~@case:inversion-simple-reflection-change, and the equality is proved.

  @case:reflection-exchange-condition The Exchange Condition follows directly
  from the Cancellation Law, using the equality of $l(w)$ and $N(w)$. Indeed,
  applying @case:reflection-word-cancellation to $w=s(0) dots s(m)$, we have
  either $s(0)=s(1)$ or else the case $i<j$ occurs, because
  $l(s(1) dots s(m))=m$. Then $s(i+1) dots s(j+1)=s(i) dots s(j)$, and we must
  have $i=0$, again because $s(1) dots s(m)$ is reduced.

  #source(577)
  @case:positive-root-stabilizer-trivial If $w!=1$, then $l(w)>0$, whence
  $N(w)>0$ by @case:reflection-length-inversion-count; then
  $w(Delta_+)!=Delta_+$ by the definition of $N(w)$, completing the argument.

  @case:longest-reflection-group-element Let $w_0$ be an element of maximal
  length. By @case:inversion-simple-reflection-change and the equality of $N(w)$
  and $l(w)$, it follows that $w_0 lr((alpha_i)) in Delta_-$ for each
  $alpha_i in Pi$. Thus $w_0 Delta_+=Delta_-$. If $w'_0$ is another element of
  maximal length, then also $w'_0 Delta_+=Delta_-$, and
  $w_0^(-1) w'_0 Delta_+=Delta_+$, so $w'_0=w_0$ by
  @case:positive-root-stabilizer-trivial. Similarly $w_0^2=1$.

  @case:reflection-right-descent follows from
  @case:inversion-simple-reflection-change and the equality of $N(w)$ and
  $l(w)$.
]

#remark(numbered: false)[
  All these amazing facts raise the question as to what extent the discussion
  depends on the choice of a particular fundamental system. This question is
  settled in Exercise~@exc:fundamental-root-systems-conjugate, where it is shown
  that any two fundamental systems are conjugate by an element of $W$.
] <rem:fundamental-system-choice>

=== Coxeter Groups <ss:coxeter-groups>

An _involution_ in a group is an element of order 2. We shall be concerned with
finite groups $W$ having a set $S$ of generators, such that each $s_i in S$ is
an involution.

#definition[
  A _finite Coxeter group_ $W$ is a finite group $W$ with a presentation
  $
    W=chevron.l s_1,...,s_n:(s_i s_j)^(m_(i j))=1
    quad "for all" i,j chevron.r,
  $
  where the ${m_(i j)}$ are positive integers such that
  $
    m_(i i)=1, quad m_(i j)>1 quad "if" i!=j, quad
    "and" m_(j i)=m_(i j) quad "for all" i,j.
  $
  We set $S={s_1,...,s_n}$, a set of involutory generators of $W$, and call the
  pair $(W,S)$ a _finite Coxeter system_.
] <def:finite-coxeter-system>
#idx("Coxeter group", target: [@def:finite-coxeter-system])
#idx("Coxeter system", target: [@def:finite-coxeter-system])
#nidx("$(W,S)$", "Coxeter system", target: [@def:finite-coxeter-system])

Our aim is to prove that every finite g.g.r., defined as in
§~@ss:finite-reflection-root-systems, is a Coxeter group. This result was first
proved by Coxeter @bib:Coxeter1934, using an ingenious topological argument,
which has been reformulated in purely group-theoretic terms by Benson–Grove
@bib:Benson1971. We shall give a proof based on the Exchange Condition
@th:reflection-length-cancellation-exchange
@case:reflection-exchange-condition, which has the advantage of also giving
presentations for Hecke algebras of certain permutation representations of
finite groups with $B N$-pairs. These results, which are due to Matsumoto
@bib:Matsumoto1964, also appear in Bourbaki @bib:Bourbaki1968, where Coxeter
systems are studied without assuming any finiteness conditions.

#definition[
  Let $W$ be a group with a finite set $S={s_1,...,s_n}$ of involutory
  generators, so every element of $W$ is a product of elements of $S$. For
  $w in W$, the _length_ $l(w)$ of $w$ is defined as the least number of factors
  needed to express $w$ as a product of elements of $S$. An expression
  $ w=s(1) dots s(m), quad "with" s(i) in S, quad 1<=i<=m, $
  #source(578) (with repetitions $s(i)=s(j)$ allowed, in general) is said to be
  _reduced_ if $l(w)=m$. The set of generators $S$ satisfies the _exchange
  condition_ if whenever
  $ l(s(1) dots s(m))=m, quad l(s(0)s(1) dots s(m))<m+1, $
  there exists an integer $k$ such that
  $ s(1) dots s(k)=s(0) dots s(k-1). $
] <def:involutory-generator-length-exchange>
#idx("Length l(w)", target: [@def:involutory-generator-length-exchange])
#idx("Exchange condition", target: [@def:involutory-generator-length-exchange])

#lemma[
  Let $D$ be a finite group generated by a pair of involutions $r,s$. Then no
  two distinct reduced expressions in $r$ and $s$ are equal in $D$, except for
  the cases:
  $ (r s)^k=(s r)^k quad "if" r s "is of even order" 2k, $
  $ (r s)^k r=(s r)^k s quad "if" r s "is of odd order" 2k+1. $
] <lem:dihedral-reduced-expression-braid>

The proof is left as an exercise. The reader will note that only expressions
with alternating factors $r$ and $s$ can be in reduced form, and that such a
group $D$ is easily shown to be isomorphic to a dihedral group, so previous
information about the generation of dihedral groups can be applied.

The next result is the theorem of Matsumoto mentioned earlier. Its statement
involves the concept of a _monoid_ $M$, which is simply a set with an
associative multiplication $(m,m') arrow.r m m'$, $m,m' in M$, with the
additional property that $M$ has an identity element $e$, satisfying $m e=e m=m$
for all $m in M$. The monoids with which we shall be concerned are either groups
or the multiplicative structures of rings.

#theorem(title: [Matsumoto @bib:Matsumoto1964])[
  Let $W$ be a finite group generated by a set of involutions $S={s_1,...,s_n}$
  satisfying the exchange condition. For each pair $(i,j)$, $i!=j$, let
  $m_(i j)$ be the order of $s_i s_j$. Then we have, for all $i!=j$,
  $
    cases(
      (s_i s_j)^(k_(i j))=(s_j s_i)^(k_(i j)) & "if" m_(i j)=2k_(i j),
      (s_i s_j)^(k_(i j)) s_i=(s_j s_i)^(k_(i j)) s_j & "if" m_(i j)=2k_(i j)+1.
    )
  $ <eq:coxeter-braid-relations>
  Let $M$ be a monoid with identity $e$, and suppose ${m_1,...,m_n}$ are
  elements of $M$ satisfying the relations @eq:coxeter-braid-relations. Then
  there exists a well-defined map $f:W arrow.r M$ such that $f(1)=e$,
  $f(s_i)=m_i$, $1<=i<=n$, and if $s_(i_1) dots s_(i_l)$ is an arbitrary reduced
  expression in $W$, then $f(s_(i_1) dots s_(i_l))=m_(i_1) dots m_(i_l)$.
] <th:matsumoto-reduced-word-monoid>
#idx("Matsumoto's Theorem", target: [@th:matsumoto-reduced-word-monoid])

#proof[
  To avoid multiple subscripts, we shall use the notation
  $ s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(l)), quad "where each" s_(i(k)) in S, $
  to denote products of elements of $S$, with repetitions allowed, and
  $m_(i(1)) dots m_(i(l))$ for the corresponding product of elements of $M$. We
  may view $1$ as the product of the #source(579) empty subset of $S$. It is
  then natural to define a map $f:W arrow.r M$ by setting
  $
    f(1)=e, quad f(s_i)=m_i, quad
    f(s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(l)))=m_(i(1)) dots m_(i(l))
  $
  for each $i$, $1<=i<=n$, and for each reduced product $s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(l))$
  of elements of $S$. We have to prove that $f$ is well defined, that is, if two
  reduced expressions represent the same element of $W$:
  $
    s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(l))=s_(j(1)) dots s_(j(l)),
    quad "with each" s in S,
  $ <eq:matsumoto-equal-reduced-words>
  then
  $ m_(i(1)) dots m_(i(l))=m_(j(1)) dots m_(j(l)). $
  (Note that the expressions in @eq:matsumoto-equal-reduced-words must have the
  same number of factors, since they are reduced.)

  We use induction on $l$, noting that the desired result is trivially true for
  $l=0,1$. Therefore we may take $l>1$, and assume the result holds for shorter
  expressions. From @eq:matsumoto-equal-reduced-words we obtain
  $ s_(j(1)) s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(l))=s_(j(2)) dots s_(j(l)), $
  and therefore the left-hand side has length $<l+1$. By the exchange condition,
  there exists an integer $k$, with $1<=k<=l$, such that
  $
    s_(j(1)) s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(k-1))=s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(k)).
  $ <eq:matsumoto-exchange-prefix>
  Substituting back in the expression @eq:matsumoto-equal-reduced-words, we
  obtain
  $
    s_(j(1)) dots s_(j(l))=s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(l))
    =s_(j(1)) s_(i(1)) dots hat(s)_(i(k)) dots s_(i(l)),
  $ <eq:matsumoto-deleted-letter-expression>
  with the factor $s_(i(k))$ deleted from the last expression. Cancelling
  $s_(j(1))$ we obtain
  $ s_(j(2)) dots s_(j(l))=s_(i(1)) dots hat(s)_(i(k)) dots s_(i(l)), $
  and the expression on the left is reduced, so both are reduced. By induction
  we have
  $ m_(j(2)) dots m_(j(l))=m_(i(1)) dots hat(m)_(i(k)) dots m_(i(l)), $
  and hence
  $
    m_(j(1)) dots m_(j(l))
    =m_(j(1)) m_(i(1)) dots hat(m)_(i(k)) dots m_(i(l)).
  $
  In the case $k<l$, we also have by induction, from
  @eq:matsumoto-exchange-prefix, since both factors are #source(580) reduced,
  $ m_(j(1)) m_(i(1)) dots m_(i(k-1))=m_(i(1)) dots m_(i(k)), $
  and can substitute this in the expression above to obtain
  $m_(i(1)) dots m_(i(l))=m_(j(1)) dots m_(j(l))$, proving the result in this
  case. In the case $k=l$, the previous inductive argument yields
  $ m_(j(1)) dots m_(j(l))=m_(j(1)) m_(i(1)) dots m_(i(l-1)). $
  In the case $k=l$, formula @eq:matsumoto-deleted-letter-expression becomes
  $
    s_(j(1)) s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(l-1))=s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(l)),
  $ <eq:matsumoto-last-letter-exception>
  and it suffices to deduce from this that
  $ m_(j(1)) m_(i(1)) dots m_(i(l-1))=m_(i(1)) dots m_(i(l)). $
  Now repeat the argument, using formula @eq:matsumoto-last-letter-exception
  instead of @eq:matsumoto-equal-reduced-words, with $i(1),...,i(l)$ playing the
  role of the $j$'s in the original discussion. The previous argument now
  establishes the result, except for what corresponds to the exceptional case
  @eq:matsumoto-last-letter-exception, which now requires us to prove that if
  $
    s_(i(1)) s_(j(1)) s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(l-2))
    =s_(j(1)) s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(l-1)),
  $
  then necessarily
  $
    m_(i(1)) m_(j(1)) m_(i(1)) dots m_(i(l-2))
    =m_(j(1)) m_(i(1)) dots m_(i(l-1)).
  $
  Continuing in this way, we either obtain the result at some stage, or arrive
  at a final exceptional case, which is to prove that the equality of two
  reduced expressions involving only the factors $s_(i(1))$ and $s_(j(1))$,
  namely
  $ s_(i(1)) s_(j(1)) s_(i(1)) dots=s_(j(1)) s_(i(1)) s_(j(1)) dots $
  implies that
  $ m_(i(1)) m_(j(1)) m_(i(1)) dots=m_(j(1)) m_(i(1)) m_(j(1)) dots. $
  By Lemma~@lem:dihedral-reduced-expression-braid, applied to the group
  $chevron.l s_(i(1)),s_(j(1)) chevron.r$, the only equality of two reduced
  expressions involving $s_(i(1))$ and $s_(j(1))$ is one of the formulas
  @eq:coxeter-braid-relations, and we have assumed in the hypothesis of the
  theorem that these relations also hold for ${m_1,...,m_n}$. This completes the
  proof.
]

#theorem[
  Let $W=W(Delta)$ be a finite g.g.r. associated with a root system $Delta$, and
  let $S={s_1,...,s_n}$ be a set of fundamental reflections defined by some
  fundamental system $Pi subset.eq Delta$ (see
  Definition~@def:fundamental-root-system). Then $(W,S)$ is a Coxeter system.
] <th:finite-reflection-group-coxeter-presentation>

#source(581)
#proof[
  By
  @th:reflection-length-cancellation-exchange
  @case:reflection-exchange-condition, the set of generators $S$ satisfies the
  exchange condition. According to Definition~@i:def:group-presentation, we have
  to prove that if $G$ is any group containing elements ${g_1,...,g_n}$ such
  that $g_i^2=1$, $(g_i g_j)^(m_(i j))=1$ for $i!=j$, where
  $m_(i j)="order of" s_i s_j$, then there exists a homomorphism $f:W arrow.r G$
  such that $f(s_i)=g_i$, $1<=i<=n$. By Matsumoto's
  Theorem~@th:matsumoto-reduced-word-monoid, there exists a map $f:W arrow.r G$
  such that $f(1)=e$, where $e$ is the identity element in $G$, $f(s_i)=g_i$,
  $1<=i<=n$, and $f(s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(l)))=g_(i(1)) dots g_(i(l))$ for all
  reduced expressions $s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(l))$. We shall prove that $f$ is a
  homomorphism, and for this it is sufficient to prove that for all $w in W$,
  and $s_i in S$, we have
  $ f(s_i w)=f(s_i) f(w). $
  Let $w=s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(k))$, $s_(i(j)) in S$, be a reduced expression, so
  $l(w)=k$. If $s_i s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(k))$ is a reduced expression, then the
  result is immediate by the properties of the map $f$. So we may assume that
  $l(s_i w)<l(w)$. Put $w'=s_i w$; then $l(s_i w')>l(w')$ by our assumption;
  hence by the first case we have $f(s_i w')=f(s_i) f(w')$. Since both $s_i$ and
  $f(s_i)$ have square 1, we have $s_i w'=w$, and $f(s_i) f(s_i w')=f(w')$,
  which is the desired result, and completes the proof of the theorem.
]

We shall next establish a converse to
Theorem~@th:finite-reflection-group-coxeter-presentation, asserting that every
finite Coxeter group can be identified with a g.g.r. acting on some euclidean
space. We also include some remarks on the classification of finite Coxeter
groups.

The first step is to define the concept of an indecomposable Coxeter system. The
natural definition of indecomposability for a root system $Delta$ and the
associated g.g.r. $W(Delta)$ is that $Delta$ cannot be decomposed as a union of
two mutually orthogonal subroot systems. Noting that for two nonproportional
roots $alpha,beta in Delta$, $(alpha,beta)=0$ if and only if the reflections
$s_alpha$ and $s_beta$ commute, we are led to the following definition:

#definition[
  Let $(W,S)$ be a finite Coxeter system. The _Coxeter graph_ associated with
  $(W,S)$ consists of a set of vertices, edges, and positive integers associated
  with edges, as follows. We identify the vertices with the elements of $S$, and
  join two vertices by an edge if and only if the corresponding elements $s,s'$
  of $S$ do not commute; in this case, we label the edge by the positive integer
  $m$, where $m$ is the order of $s s'$ in $W$. The Coxeter system is called
  _indecomposable_ if and only if the Coxeter graph is connected.
] <def:coxeter-graph-indecomposable>
#idx("Coxeter graph", target: [@def:coxeter-graph-indecomposable])
#idx(
  "Indecomposable Coxeter system",
  target: [@def:coxeter-graph-indecomposable],
)

Note that the condition for $s$ and $s'$ to be joined by an edge in the Coxeter
graph is equivalent to the statement that $m>2$, where $m$ is the order of
$s s'$, since two distinct involutions in a group commute if and only if their
product has order 2. We also remark that the presentation
@def:finite-coxeter-system of a finite Coxeter system $(W,S)$ can be read from
the Coxeter graph; for example,
#coxeter-example()
#source(582) is the Coxeter graph of the Coxeter group
$
  chevron.l s_1,s_2,s_3:s_1^2=s_2^2=s_3^2=(s_1 s_2)^3
  =(s_2 s_3)^4=(s_1 s_3)^2=1 chevron.r.
$
In @prop:coxeter-connected-component-product we shall see that every finite
Coxeter group is isomorphic to a direct product of indecomposable ones.

The classification of Coxeter groups is reduced to the classification of root
systems by the next result, which asserts that every finite Coxeter group is
isomorphic to a g.g.r. This means, by
Theorem~@th:finite-reflection-group-coxeter-presentation, that the families of
finite Coxeter groups and finite g.g.r.'s can be identified with one another.

#theorem[
  Let $(W,S)$ be a finite Coxeter system, with generators $S={s_1,...,s_n}$. Let
  $V$ be a vector space of dimension $n$ over $RR$, and let ${e_1,...,e_n}$ be a
  basis of $V$. Define a bilinear form $B:V times V arrow.r RR$ by setting
  $ B(e_i,e_j)=-cos(pi/m_(i j)), quad 1<=i,j<=n, $
  where $m_(i j)$ is the order of $s_i s_j$ in $W$. For each $s_i in S$, define
  a linear transformation $T(s_i)$ on $V$ by setting
  $ T(s_i)e_j=e_j-2B(e_j,e_i)e_i, quad 1<=j<=n. $
  Then the following statements hold:
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [#case[
      The map $T:S arrow.r End V$ can be extended to a faithful representation
      $T:W arrow.r GL(V)$.
    ] <case:coxeter-geometric-representation-faithful>],
    [#case[
      The bilinear form $B$ is symmetric, positive definite, and invariant with
      respect to $T(w)$ for all $w in W$.
    ] <case:coxeter-geometric-positive-definite-form>],
    [#case[
      The group $T(W)$ is a finite g.g.r., and ${T(s_1),...,T(s_n)}$ can be
      identified with a set of fundamental reflections in $T(W)$.
    ] <case:coxeter-geometric-fundamental-reflections>],
    [#case[
      If $(W,S)$ is an indecomposable Coxeter system, then the representation
      $T$ is absolutely irreducible.
    ] <case:coxeter-geometric-absolutely-irreducible>],
  )
] <th:finite-coxeter-geometric-representation>

#proof[
  (See Bourbaki @bib:Bourbaki1968, Chapter V, and Deodhar @bib:Deodhar1982). We
  have $m_(i i)=1$, and $m_(i j)>=2$ for $i!=j$, since $s_i^2=1$ and since we
  may assume the elements of $S$ are distinct. It follows that $B(e_i,e_i)=1$,
  $1<=i<=n$, and that the restriction of $B$ to the subspace
  $chevron.l e_i,e_j chevron.r$ is nondegenerate, for all $i!=j$, $1<=i,j<=n$.
  Then we have
  $
    V=chevron.l e_i chevron.r plus.o chevron.l e_i chevron.r^perp
    quad "for" 1<=i<=n,
  $
  and
  $
    V=chevron.l e_i,e_j chevron.r plus.o
    chevron.l e_i,e_j chevron.r^perp quad "for" i!=j, quad 1<=i,j<=n.
  $
  (The notation $chevron.l v,w,... chevron.r$ denotes the subspace of $V$
  generated by $v,w$, etc., and $perp$ #source(583) denotes orthogonality with
  respect to the form $B$.) Using the definition of $T(s_i)$ and the first
  decomposition of $V$, we see that $T(s_i)^2=1$, $1<=i<=n$. The second implies
  that $T(s_i)T(s_j)$ acts trivially on $chevron.l e_i,e_j chevron.r^perp$ if
  $i!=j$, and leaves $chevron.l e_i,e_j chevron.r$ invariant. An easy
  computation shows that $(T(s_i)T(s_j))^(m_(i j))=1$ on
  $chevron.l e_i,e_j chevron.r$. It follows from the universal property of the
  defining relations of $W$ that $T$ can be extended to a representation
  $T:W arrow.r GL(V)$.

  A computation shows that
  $ B(T(s_i)e_j,T(s_i)e_k)=B(e_j,e_k), quad 1<=i,j,k<=n, $
  and hence the form $B$ is invariant with respect to $T(w)$ for all $w in W$.
  It is also clear that $B$ is symmetric.

  Label the vertices of the Coxeter graph of $(W,S)$ by the integers
  ${1,...,n}$, in such a way that ${1,...,n_1}$, ${n_1+1,...,n_1+n_2}$, etc.,
  correspond to the connected components of the graph. Let $V_1,...,V_k$ be the
  subspaces of $V$ spanned by ${e_1,...,e_(n_1)}$,
  ${e_(n_1+1),...,e_(n_1+n_2)}$, etc., and let $S_1,S_2,...,S_k$ be the
  corresponding subsets of $S$. Note that the order of $s_i s_j$ in $W$ is 2 if
  and only if $B(e_i,e_j)=0$. It follows from the definition of the Coxeter
  graph that $V_i subset V_j^perp$ for all $i!=j$, and hence
  $ V=V_1 plus.o dots plus.o V_k. $
  Moreover, we have
  $ T(s_i)V_j subset.eq V_j, quad 1<=i<=n, quad 1<=j<=k. $
  We now prove that each subspace $V_i$ is an absolutely simple $RR W$-module,
  with the $W$-action defined by the restriction of the representation $T$ to
  $V_i$.

  Consider a linear map $X:V arrow.r V$ that commutes with all the
  ${T(w):w in W}$. For each $i$, $1<=i<=n$, the decomposition
  $V=chevron.l e_i chevron.r plus.o chevron.l e_i chevron.r^perp$
  implies that $chevron.l e_i chevron.r$ is the $(-1)$-eigenspace for $T(s_i)$,
  and $chevron.l e_i chevron.r^perp$ is the $(+1)$-eigenspace for $T(s_i)$. It
  follows that $X$ leaves the subspaces $chevron.l e_i chevron.r$ and
  $chevron.l e_i chevron.r^perp$ invariant for each $i$, and hence there exist
  elements $lambda_i in RR$ such that
  $ X e_i=lambda_i e_i, quad 1<=i<=n. $
  Now suppose $s_i s_j$ has order $>2$, so that $i$ and $j$ belong to the same
  connected component of the Coxeter graph. Then $B(e_i,e_j)!=0$, and we have
  $ T(s_i)e_j=e_j+a_(j i)e_i, quad "where" a_(j i)!=0. $
  Since $X$ and $T(s_i)$ commute, we have $X T(s_i)e_j=lambda_j T(s_i)e_j$, and
  $X(e_j+a_(j i)e_i)=lambda_j e_j+a_(j i)lambda_i e_i$. It follows that
  $lambda_i=lambda_j$, since $a_(j i)!=0$. Thus $X$ acts as a scalar
  multiplication on each of the subspaces $V_i$, $1<=i<=k$, and by
  @i:th:absolute-simplicity-centralizer and @bib:CurtisReiner1962[CR] Exercise
  27.1, it follows that each submodule $V_i$ is absolutely simple. This proves
  part~@case:coxeter-geometric-absolutely-irreducible.

  #source(584) Now consider one of the absolutely simple modules $V_i$ for a
  fixed $i$, where $1<=i<=k$, and let $B_i$ be the restriction of $B$ to $V_i$.
  Since $W$ is a finite group, there exists a $T(W)$-invariant positive definite
  symmetric bilinear form $B'_i$ on $V_i$, which can be constructed using the
  averaging process (see @bib:CurtisReiner1962[CR] Exercise 10.6). Then $B'_i$
  identifies $V_i$ with its dual, and we have
  $ B_i lr((u,v))=B'_i lr((u,X v)), quad u,v in V_i, $
  for some $X in End_RR V_i$. Since both $B_i$ and $B'_i$ are $T(W)$-invariant,
  it follows that $X T(w)=T(w)X$ for all $w in W$. Because $V_i$ is absolutely
  simple, $X=lambda dot 1$ for some $lambda in RR$, and we have
  $B_i=lambda B'_i$; further, $lambda>0$ since $B(e_i,e_i)=1$ for each $i$. It
  follows that $B_i$ is positive definite on each space $V_i$, and hence $B$ is
  positive definite on $V$, completing the proof of
  @case:coxeter-geometric-positive-definite-form.

  At this point we can identify $T(W)$ with a finite g.g.r. acting on $V$, with
  the inner product given by $B$. Then the vectors ${e_1,...,e_n}$ and their
  images ${T(w)e_i:w in W,1<=i<=n}$ form a root system $Delta$ associated with
  $T(W)$. We now prove, following Deodhar @bib:Deodhar1982, that ${e_1,...,e_n}$
  is a fundamental system in $Delta$; this will imply that ${T(s_1),...,T(s_n)}$
  is a set of fundamental reflections in $T(W)$, by definition of the
  ${T(s_i)}$.#ed-note[
    The original proof compresses the step from the rank-two calculation to a
    fundamental system. Positivity must be established from abstract word length
    before the image can be identified faithfully with $W$; the later use of
    Theorem~@th:reflection-length-cancellation-exchange is then legitimate.
  ]

  By Definition~@def:fundamental-root-system, it suffices to prove that
  $T(w)e_i$ is a nonnegative combination of the basis vectors whenever
  $l(w s_i)>l(w)$. Here $l$ always denotes length in the original abstract
  generating set $S$. The map $epsilon:S arrow.r plus.minus 1$ defined by
  $epsilon(s_j)=-1$ preserves the defining relations @def:finite-coxeter-system,
  and extends to a homomorphism $epsilon:W arrow.r plus.minus 1$. Thus
  $epsilon(w)=(-1)^(l(w))$, and the length inequalities and parity give
  $ l(w s_j)=l(w)plus.minus 1 quad "for all" s_j in S. $

  We use induction on $l(w)$; the assertion is clear for $w=1$. Suppose $l(w)>0$
  and $l(w s_i)=l(w)+1$. A reduced expression for $w$ has a last factor $s_j$,
  with $l(w s_j)=l(w)-1$; necessarily $j!=i$. Put
  $D=chevron.l s_i,s_j chevron.r$ and $m=m_(i j)$, and write $l_D$ for intrinsic
  length in these two generators. Since $s_i s_j$ has order $m$, $D$ is
  dihedral. Its reduced pair-words alternate, are unique at lengths less than
  $m$, and the two alternating words of length $m$ coincide, by
  Lemma~@lem:dihedral-reduced-expression-braid. In particular, its lengths are
  $0,...,m$ and an intrinsic ascent changes length by $1$.

  Starting at $w$, repeatedly remove a right descent among $s_i,s_j$, beginning
  with $s_j$. If the removed generators are $h_1,...,h_k$, put
  $ x=w h_1 dots h_k, quad y=h_k dots h_1. $
  The process terminates, since length decreases by $1$ at every step. Then
  $k>=1$, $w=x y$, $l(x)=l(w)-k<l(w)$, and both $s_i,s_j$ are right ascents of
  $x$. Since $y$ has an expression with $k$ pair-generators,
  $
    l(w)<=l(x)+l(y)<=l(x)+l_D lr((y))<=l(x)+k=l(w).
  $
  Hence every inequality is an equality, in particular $l(y)=l_D lr((y))=k$. If
  $l_D lr((y s_i))=k-1$, then
  $ l(w s_i)<=l(x)+l_D lr((y s_i))=l(w)-1, $
  a contradiction. Consequently $l_D lr((y s_i))=k+1$, so $k<m$ and the
  alternating reduced pair-word for $y$ ends in $s_j$. This construction uses
  neither exchange in $W$ nor a shortest representative of $w D$.

  For completeness, set $theta=pi/m$, $c=cos theta$ and
  $q_a=sin(a theta)/sin theta$. We have $sin theta>0$, $q_0=0$, $q_1=1$, and
  $q_(a+1)=2 c q_a-q_(a-1)$. On the pair plane the definitions give
  $
    T(s_i)e_i=-e_i, quad T(s_i)e_j=e_j+2 c e_i,
    quad T(s_j)e_i=e_i+2 c e_j, quad T(s_j)e_j=-e_j.
  $
  For the alternating word $y$ of length $k$ ending in $s_j$, these formulas
  give $T(y)e_i=q_(k+1)e_i+q_k e_j$ if $k$ is even, and
  $T(y)e_i=q_k e_i+q_(k+1)e_j$ if $k$ is odd. Indeed, the even formula starts at
  $k=0$; prepend $s_j$ to pass from even to odd length, and prepend $s_i$ to
  pass from odd to even length, using the recurrence. Since $0<=k<m$, both
  coefficients are nonnegative. At $k=m-1$ one coefficient is $q_m=0$ and the
  other is $q_(m-1)=1$. The same formulas include the commuting case $m=2$,
  where $k=0$ or $1$. The vector is nonzero, since $T(y)$ is invertible.

  We have therefore proved $T(y)e_i=a e_i+b e_j$ with $a,b>=0$. Both $s_i,s_j$
  are ascents of the strictly shorter element $x$, so induction gives that
  $T(x)e_i$ and $T(x)e_j$ are nonnegative combinations of the basis vectors.
  Hence so is
  $ T(w)e_i=a T(x)e_i+b T(x)e_j. $
  If instead $l(w s_i)=l(w)-1$, put $v=w s_i$. Then $s_i$ is an ascent of $v$
  and $T(w)e_i=-T(v)e_i$ is nonzero and nonpositive. Thus every root has one
  sign; the vectors are nonzero because all $T(w)$ are invertible. This proves
  that ${e_1,...,e_n}$ is a fundamental system in $Delta$, and establishes
  part~@case:coxeter-geometric-fundamental-reflections.

  Only now do we deduce the stronger assertion about a shortest pair-coset
  representative. If $x$ has no right descent among $s_i,s_j$, then both
  $T(x)e_i$ and $T(x)e_j$ are positive. Along an intrinsically reduced pair-word
  $z$, the same sine formulas, with $i,j$ interchanged when necessary, make the
  next simple-root image a nonnegative pair-combination. Applying $T(x)$
  preserves positivity. By the ascent and descent criteria just proved, each
  successive multiplication is therefore an ascent in $W$. It follows that
  $l(x z)=l(x)+l_D lr((z))$ for every $z in D$. Thus $x$ is the unique shortest
  element of $x D=w D$. This consequence is not used in the positivity
  induction.

  #source(585) It remains to prove that the representation $T$ is faithful. Let
  $w!=1$, and choose a reduced expression $w=u s_i$. Then $s_i$ is an ascent of
  $u$, so $T(u)e_i$ is nonzero and positive by what was just proved. But
  $T(w)e_i=-T(u)e_i$ is negative and cannot equal $e_i$. Consequently $T(w)!=1$,
  proving $ker T=1$. With this faithful identification,
  @th:reflection-length-cancellation-exchange also gives
  $l(w)=N(w)=abs(Delta_w^-)$, where
  $Delta_w^-={alpha in Delta_+:T(w)alpha in Delta_-}$ and $Delta_+$ is the
  positive system determined by $Pi={e_1,...,e_n}$. This completes the proof of
  the theorem.
]

#definition[
  Let $(W,S)$ be an indecomposable Coxeter system. The absolutely irreducible
  representation $T$ defined in @th:finite-coxeter-geometric-representation is
  called the _reflection representation_ of $W$.
] <def:coxeter-reflection-representation>
#idx(
  "Reflection representation",
  target: [@def:coxeter-reflection-representation],
)

#proposition[
  Let $(W,S)$ be a finite Coxeter system, and let $S_1,...,S_k$ be the subsets
  of $S$ associated with the connected components of the Coxeter graph of $W$.
  Let $W_i=chevron.l S_i chevron.r$, $1<=i<=k$. Then $(W_i,S_i)$ is a finite
  Coxeter system for each $i$, and we have
  $ W=W_1 times dots times W_k quad ("direct product"). $
] <prop:coxeter-connected-component-product>

The proof is left as an exercise for the reader.

The classification of indecomposable Coxeter systems is given in Bourbaki
@bib:Bourbaki1968. By Theorem~@th:finite-coxeter-geometric-representation, the
problem comes down to the classification of root systems associated with
indecomposable g.g.r.'s. A list of the Coxeter graphs of indecomposable g.g.r.'s
whose root systems satisfy the crystallographic condition
#coxeter-crystallographic()

#source(586) is given above, using the notation of E. Cartan. In the list,
unlabeled edges are assigned the integer 3. The classification of the
corresponding root systems can be found in Bourbaki @bib:Bourbaki1968, or in any
book on Lie algebras, and will not be repeated here. There is, up to a change of
scale, a unique root system of each type, except in the case of $B_n$, $C_n$,
where there are two. The first step in the classification, which asserts that
the Coxeter graph of an indecomposable Coxeter system is a tree, is given in
Exercise~@exc:finite-coxeter-graph-tree. The classification of root systems of
types $A_n$, $D_n$, $E_n$ is sketched in
§~@sec:graph-representations-gabriel-theorem.

=== Parabolic Subgroups of Finite Coxeter Groups
<ss:finite-coxeter-parabolic-subgroups>

In this subsection, $(W,S)$ denotes an arbitrary finite Coxeter system (see
@def:finite-coxeter-system). By
Theorem~@th:finite-coxeter-geometric-representation, there exists a root system
$Delta$ in a euclidean space such that $W$ can be identified with the g.g.r.
associated with $Delta$, and $S$ with a set of fundamental reflections. Thus the
results of @th:reflection-length-cancellation-exchange can be applied to the
pair $(W,S)$. In particular, we recall the following results for the convenience
of the reader; these are the only ones required for most of this subsection.

#theorem(name: "Cancellation Law")[
  Let
  $ w=s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(m)) in W, quad "where each" s_(i(j)) in S, $
  and assume that we have $l(w)<m$. Then there exist integers $j<=k$ such that
  $ w=s_(i(1)) dots hat(s)_(i(j)) dots hat(s)_(i(k+1)) dots s_(i(m)). $
] <th:coxeter-cancellation-law>
#idx("Cancellation law", target: [@th:coxeter-cancellation-law])

#theorem(name: "Exchange Condition")[
  Let
  $
    l(s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(m)))=m quad "and"
    l(s_(i(0)) s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(m)))<m+1.
  $
  Then for some $j$, $0<=j<=m-1$,
  $ s_(i(0)) dots s_(i(j))=s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(j+1)). $
] <th:coxeter-exchange-condition>
#idx("Exchange condition", target: [@th:coxeter-exchange-condition])

As a first application of these results, we have

#lemma[
  Let
  $ s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(m))=s_(j(1)) dots s_(j(m)), quad "with each" s in S, $
  be two reduced expressions of length $m$. Then
  $ {i(1),...,i(m)}={j(1),...,j(m)}; $
  in other words, the subsets of $S$ supporting the two expressions coincide.
] <lem:reduced-word-generator-support>

#source(587)
#proof[
  We use induction on $m$. The result is clear if $m=1$, and so we assume $m>1$.
  From the hypothesis we have
  $ s_(i(2)) dots s_(i(m))=s_(i(1)) s_(j(1)) dots s_(j(m)). $
  It follows that $l(s_(j(1)) dots s_(j(m)))=m$, and
  $l(s_(i(1)) s_(j(1)) dots s_(j(m)))<m+1$, so by @th:coxeter-exchange-condition
  we have (for some $k$)
  $
    s_(i(2)) dots s_(i(m))=s_(i(1)) s_(j(1)) dots s_(j(m))
    =s_(j(1)) dots hat(s)_(j(k)) dots s_(j(m)).
  $
  By the induction hypothesis, we obtain
  $ {i(2),...,i(m)} subset.eq {j(1),...,j(m)}. $
  We also have, from the above formula,
  $
    s_(i(1))=s_(j(1)) dots hat(s)_(j(k)) dots s_(j(m))
    s_(j(m)) dots s_(j(1)).
  $
  The right-hand side has length 1, and therefore, by successive applications of
  the Cancellation Law~@th:coxeter-cancellation-law, it follows that
  $i(1) in {j(1),...,j(m)}$, and we have shown that
  $ {i(1),...,i(m)} subset.eq {j(1),...,j(m)}. $
  The reverse inclusion is proved similarly, and the result follows.
]

The next result, which is an easy application of the preceding lemma, introduces
an important family of subgroups of $W$.

#proposition[
  Let $J$ be an arbitrary subset of the fundamental reflections
  $S={s_1,...,s_n}$, and let $W_J$ denote the subgroup $chevron.l J chevron.r$
  of $W$ generated by the involutions in $J$. Then for each $w in W_J$, all
  reduced expressions for $w$ have all their factors in $J$.
] <prop:standard-parabolic-reduced-words>

#proof[
  Let $w$ be an arbitrary element of $W_J$. Then $w$ has at least one reduced
  expression with all factors in $J$, by successive applications of the
  cancellation law. The fact that all reduced expressions for $w$ have the same
  property follows at once from @lem:reduced-word-generator-support, completing
  the proof.
]

#definition[
  Let $(W,S)$ be a finite Coxeter system. The subgroups ${W_J}_(J subset.eq S)$,
  and their conjugates, are called _parabolic subgroups_ of $W$. For a fixed set
  of generators $S$, the subgroups of the form ${W_J}_(J subset.eq S)$ are
  called _standard parabolic subgroups_.
] <def:coxeter-parabolic-subgroup>
#idx("Parabolic subgroups", target: [@def:coxeter-parabolic-subgroup])
#nidx(
  "${W_J}, {P_J}$",
  "parabolic subgroups of W and G, respectively",
  target: [@def:coxeter-parabolic-subgroup],
)

#proposition[
  Let $W$ be a finite g.g.r. associated with a root system $Delta$, let $Pi$ be
  a set of fundamental roots, and let $S$ be the set of fundamental reflections.
  Let #source(588) $J subset.eq S$, and let $Pi_J$ be the corresponding subset
  of $Pi$. Set $Delta_J=Delta inter chevron.l Pi_J chevron.r$.
  ($chevron.l Pi_J chevron.r$ is the subspace of the underlying vector space $V$
  generated by $Pi_J$.) Then $Delta_J$ is a root system in
  $chevron.l Pi_J chevron.r$ with fundamental system $Pi_J$, and $W_J$ is the
  g.g.r. associated with $Delta_J$.
] <prop:parabolic-root-subsystem>

#proof[
  By @th:finite-reflection-group-coxeter-presentation, the pair $(W,S)$ is a
  Coxeter system. It is easily checked that $Delta_J$ satisfies the axioms
  @def:root-system for a root system. Since $Delta_J$ is contained in the
  subspace of $V$ generated by the linearly independent set $Pi_J$, it is clear
  from @def:fundamental-root-system that $Pi_J$ is a fundamental system in
  $Delta_J$. Finally, by Theorem~@th:fundamental-reflections-generate, the
  g.g.r. associated with $Delta_J$ is generated by the reflections corresponding
  to the roots in $Pi_J$, and it follows that this g.g.r. coincides with $W_J$,
  completing the proof.
]

The preceding result shows that the parabolic subgroups of $W$ are themselves
g.g.r.'s associated with subroot systems of $Delta$. As shown in the exercises,
however, there exist, in general, subroot systems of $Delta$ whose g.g.r.'s are
not parabolic subgroups of $W$. Their study is a subtle business and is needed
for the determination of the conjugacy classes in a Weyl group (Carter
@bib:Carter1972a).

#proposition[
  Let $(W,S)$ be a finite Coxeter system. Then the following statements hold:
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [#case[
      For $J subset.eq S$, the length $l(w)$ of an element of $W_J$ is the same
      whether $w$ is viewed as an element of $W$, or of the subgroup $W_J$ with
      the set of involutory generators $J$, as in
      @prop:standard-parabolic-reduced-words.
    ] <case:parabolic-intrinsic-length>],
    [#case[
      For each $J subset.eq S$, the pair $(W_J,J)$ is a Coxeter system.
    ] <case:standard-parabolic-coxeter-system>],
    [#case[
      The map $J arrow.r W_J$, $J subset.eq S$, is a bijection from the family
      of all subsets of $S$ to a family of subgroups of $W$.
    ] <case:standard-parabolic-subset-bijection>],
    [#case[For all $I,J subset.eq S$, $W_I inter W_J=W_(I inter J)$.]
      <case:standard-parabolic-intersection>],
    [#case[$S$ is a minimal set of generators of $W$.]
      <case:coxeter-generators-minimal>],
  )
] <prop:standard-parabolic-properties>

The proofs are all easy consequences of the preceding results, and are left as
exercises.

By @prop:standard-parabolic-properties@case:standard-parabolic-intersection, the
intersection of two standard parabolic subgroups $W_I$ and $W_J$ is a parabolic
subgroup $W_(I inter J)$. The last result in this section is that the
intersection of any two parabolic subgroups is parabolic (that is, a conjugate
of subgroup of the form $W_I$, $I subset.eq S$.) This is a somewhat surprising
result, especially in view of
Exercise~@exc:reflection-subgroup-intersection-not-reflection, which shows that
the intersection of two g.g.r.'s is not necessarily a g.g.r. We first require a
preliminary result.

#proposition[
  Let $I,J subset.eq S$. Every double coset
  $W_I w W_J in W_I backslash W "/" W_J$ contains a unique element $x$ of
  minimal length. This element has the further property that each element in
  $W_I x W_J$ has an expression $u x v$, with $u in W_I$, $v in W_J$, and
  $ l(u x v)=l(u)+l(x)+l(v). $
] <prop:parabolic-double-coset-minimal-length>

#source(589)
#proof[
  Let $x in W_I w W_J$ be an element of minimal length $m$ in the double coset,
  and let $u x v$ be another element in the double coset, with $u in W_I$,
  $v in W_J$. We shall prove that if $l(u x v)=m$, then $u x v=x$. If, for
  reduced expressions for $u,x,v$, the resulting expression for $u x v$ is also
  reduced, then $l(u x v)=m$ implies $u=v=1$, since $l(x)=m$ by assumption. If
  the expression obtained for $u x v$ is not reduced, then by the Cancellation
  Law we have $u x v=u_1 x_1 v_1$, with $u_1,x_1,v_1$ obtained from $u,x,v$,
  respectively, by cancelling factors; further, $u_1 in W_I$ and $v_1 in W_J$,
  since by @prop:standard-parabolic-reduced-words any reduced expression for $u$
  has all its factors in $I$, and similarly those of $v$ lie in $J$. Then
  $x_1 in W_I x W_J$, and if $x_1!=x$, then $x_1$ is an element of the double
  coset of shorter length than $x$, contrary to the choice of $x$. So we have
  $u x v=u_1 x v_1$, and continuing the cancellation process if necessary, we
  obtain a reduced expression for $u x v$ of the form $u' x v'$, with $u'$ and
  $v'$ reduced. Since $l(u' x v')=m$, we have $u'=v'=1$, which proves the
  uniqueness of $x$. For the second statement, let $u x v in W_I x W_J$, with
  $u in W_I$ and $v in W_J$. By the above argument, we can obtain a reduced
  expression for $u x v$ of the form $u_1 x v_1$, with $u_1$ a reduced
  expression in $W_I$ and $v_1$ a reduced expression in $W_J$. Then
  $l(u x v)=l(u_1 x v_1)=l(u_1)+l(x)+l(v_1)$, completing the proof.
]

#definition[
  We call an element $x$ of minimal length in a double coset $W_I w W_J$, as in
  @prop:parabolic-double-coset-minimal-length, a _distinguished double coset
  representative_ (d.d.c.r.). Let $D_(I J)$ denote the set of d.d.c.r.'s for all
  double cosets in $W_I backslash W "/" W_J$.
] <def:distinguished-parabolic-double-coset-representative>
#idx(
  "Distinguished double coset representative",
  target: [@def:distinguished-parabolic-double-coset-representative],
)
#nidx(
  "$D_(I J)$",
  "set of distinguished double coset representatives",
  target: [@def:distinguished-parabolic-double-coset-representative],
)

Note, in particular, that for each $I subset.eq S$, $D_(I emptyset)$ is a set of
coset representatives for right cosets $W_I w$, and each element in
$D_(I emptyset)$ is characterized as the unique element of minimal length in its
coset. If $x in D_(I emptyset)$, and $u in W_I$ then $l(u x)=l(u)+l(x)$ by
@prop:parabolic-double-coset-minimal-length. Similar statements can be made, of
course, for $D_(emptyset J)$, where $J subset.eq S$. It is easily shown that
$D_(I J)=D_(I emptyset) inter D_(emptyset J)$.

#theorem(title: [Kilmoyer @bib:Kilmoyer1969; Solomon @bib:Solomon1976])[
  The intersection of any two parabolic subgroups of $W$ is a parabolic
  subgroup. More precisely, any intersection of conjugates of $W_I$ and $W_J$ is
  a conjugate of $W_I inter attach(W_J, tl: x)$ for some $x in D_(I J)$, and we
  have
  $ W_I inter attach(W_J, tl: x)=W_K, quad "if" x in D_(I J), $
  where $K=I inter attach(J, tl: x)$.
] <th:parabolic-subgroup-intersection>

#proof[
  First note that for $y,z in W$, the subgroup
  $attach(W_I, tl: y) inter attach(W_J, tl: z)$ is conjugate to
  $W_I inter attach(W_J, tl: w)$, where $w=y^(-1)z$. Further, if $x in D_(I J)$
  is the d.d.c.r. in $W_I w W_J$, we may write $w=u x v$ with $u in W_I$,
  $v in W_J$; then
  $W_I inter attach(W_J, tl: w)=attach((W_I inter attach(W_J, tl: x)), tl: u)$.

  Now let $x in D_(I J)$, and let $K=I inter attach(J, tl: x)$. We clearly have
  $W_K subset.eq W_I inter attach(W_J, tl: x)$, so it is sufficient to prove
  that if $w in W_I inter attach(W_J, tl: x)$, then $w in W_K$. The result is
  obvious if $l(w)=0$, so we assume $l(w)>0$, and assume, as an induction
  hypothesis, that the result holds for shorter elements of
  $W_I inter attach(W_J, tl: x)$. Because $w in W_I$ and $w!=1$, we have
  $l(s w)<l(w)$ for some $s in I$. We also have $w=x v x^(-1)$, for $v in W_J$,
  so $w x=x v$. Then $x in D_(I J)$ implies $x in D_(I emptyset)$, and
  $ l(s x v)=l(s w x)=l(s w)+l(x)<l(w)+l(x)=l(w x)=l(x v). $

  #source(590) Now let
  $
    x=s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(p)), quad "and"
    v=s_(i(p+1)) dots s_(i(p+q)),
  $
  be reduced expressions. By @prop:standard-parabolic-reduced-words,
  ${s_(i(p+1)),...,s_(i(p+q))} subset.eq J$. As $x in D_(emptyset J)$, it
  follows that
  $ x v=s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(p)) s_(i(p+1)) dots s_(i(p+q)) $
  is a reduced expression, and so $l(s x v)<l(x v)$ implies that
  $ s x v=s_(i(1)) dots hat(s)_(i(j)) dots s_(i(p+q)), $
  by the Exchange Condition. If $j<=p$, then we may cancel $v$ from both sides,
  and obtain
  $ s x=s_(i(1)) dots hat(s)_(i(j)) dots s_(i(p)) in W_I x W_J, $
  and $l(s x)<l(x)$, which is impossible. Therefore $j>=p+1$, and we have
  $x^(-1)s x in W_J$ (remembering that $x=s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(p))$). We obtain
  $ 1+l(x)=l(s x)=l(x x^(-1)s x)=l(x)+l(x^(-1)s x), $
  because $x^(-1)s x in W_J$ and $x in D_(emptyset J)$. Thus $l(x^(-1)s x)=1$,
  and $x^(-1)s x in S inter W_J=J$. Then $s in I inter attach(J, tl: x)=K$, and
  $ w=s(s w), quad l(s w)<l(w), $
  so $s w in W_I inter attach(W_J, tl: x)$. By the induction hypothesis
  $s w in W_K$, and hence $w in W_K$, as required.
]

#corollary[
  Let $W$ be a finite g.g.r. associated with a root system $Delta$, let $Pi$ be
  a set of fundamental roots, and $S$ the set of fundamental reflections, as in
  @prop:parabolic-root-subsystem. Let $Pi_I$, $Pi_J$ be the subsets of $Pi$
  corresponding to subsets $I,J subset.eq S$. Then for $x in D_(I J)$,
  $
    Pi_I inter x Pi_J=Pi_K, quad "and"
    Delta_I inter x Delta_J=Delta_K,
  $
  where $K=I inter attach(J, tl: x)$ as in @th:parabolic-subgroup-intersection.
] <cor:parabolic-root-system-intersection>

#proof[
  Let $alpha in Pi_K$; then $s_alpha in K subset.eq I inter attach(J, tl: x)$,
  and it follows that $alpha in Pi_I$, and
  $x^(-1)s_alpha x=s_(x^(-1)alpha) in J$, so $x^(-1)alpha in Pi_J$ since
  $x^(-1)alpha>0$ for $x in D_(I J)$. Conversely, $alpha in Pi_I inter x Pi_J$
  implies $alpha=x beta$, for $beta in Pi_J$, so
  $s_alpha=x s_beta x^(-1) in W_I inter attach(W_J, tl: x)=W_K$ by
  @th:parabolic-subgroup-intersection. Then $s_alpha in K$ since $l(s_alpha)=1$,
  and the only elements in $W_K$ of length 1 are the elements of $K$, by
  @prop:standard-parabolic-properties@case:parabolic-intrinsic-length. For the
  second statement, we have $Delta_K=W_K Pi_K$ by
  @th:fundamental-reflections-generate applied to $W_K$. Then
  $
    W_K Pi_K subset.eq W_I Pi_I inter attach(W_J, tl: x) x Pi_J
    subset.eq Delta_I inter x Delta_J.
  $
  #source(591) Conversely, if $gamma in Delta_I inter x Delta_J$, then
  $s_gamma in W_I inter attach(W_J, tl: x)=W_K$ by
  @th:parabolic-subgroup-intersection. But $s_gamma in W_K$ implies
  $gamma in Delta_K$, since $W_K$ is the g.g.r. of $Delta_K$ by
  @prop:parabolic-root-subsystem, and the proof is completed.
]

#exercises-heading

#exercise[
  Let $n$ be a positive integer, and let ${e_1,...,e_(n+1)}$ be an orthonormal
  basis of $RR^(n+1)$, with respect to the usual inner product. Let $Delta$ be
  the set of all vectors $alpha=e_i-e_j$, $i!=j$, $1<=i,j<=n+1$, and let $V$
  denote the hyperplane in $RR^(n+1)$ consisting of all vectors $sum a_i e_i$
  with $sum a_i=0$.
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [#case[
      Prove that $Delta$ is a root system in $V$, satisfying the
      crystallographic condition, whose associated g.g.r. $W(Delta)$ is
      isomorphic to the symmetric group $S_(n+1)$. Show that the reflections
      ${s_alpha:alpha in Delta}$ in $RR^(n+1)$ permute the basis vectors
      ${e_i}$, so their restrictions to $V$ generate $W(Delta)$ and define the
      isomorphism $W(Delta) tilde.eq S_(n+1)$.
    ] <case:type-a-roots-symmetric-group>],
    [#case[
      Show that the set of roots ${e_i-e_j:i<j}$ is a positive set of roots in
      $V$ containing the fundamental system $Pi={alpha_1,...,alpha_n}$, where
      $alpha_i=e_i-e_(i+1)$, $1<=i<=n$.
    ] <case:type-a-positive-simple-roots>],
    [#case[
      Let $S={s_(alpha_1),...,s_(alpha_n)}$. Verify that $(W(Delta),S)$ is an
      indecomposable Coxeter system whose Coxeter graph is of type $A_n$.
    ] <case:type-a-coxeter-graph>],
    [#case[
      Let $J subset.eq S$, and let $W_J$ be the corresponding standard parabolic
      subgroup of $W(Delta)$. Prove that $W_J$ is isomorphic to a subgroup of
      the symmetric group $S_(n+1)$ of the form
      $S_(n_1) times dots times S_(n_t)$, for some partition ${n_1,...,n_t}$ of
      $n+1$ (see also the definition of Young subgroups in
      §~@ss:specht-modules-simple-symmetric-modules).
    ] <case:type-a-parabolic-young-subgroups>],
  )
] <exc:type-a-root-system>

#exercise[
  Let ${e_1,...,e_n}$ be an orthonormal basis of $RR^n$, as in
  Exercise~@exc:type-a-root-system, and let $Delta$ denote the set of vectors
  ${plus.minus e_i,plus.minus e_i plus.minus e_j:i!=j,1<=i,j<=n}$.
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [#case[
      Prove that $Delta$ is a root system in $RR^n$, satisfying the
      crystallographic condition whose associated g.g.r. is isomorphic to the
      _hyperoctahedral group_ $H_n$. (The group $H_n$ is defined as the wreath
      product $ZZ_2 wreath S_n$ (see §~@i:ss:tensor-induction), and is
      isomorphic to the symmetry group of the $n$-dimensional cube.)
    ] <case:type-b-roots-hyperoctahedral-group>],
    [#case[
      Prove that $Pi={e_1-e_2,...,e_(n-1)-e_n,e_n}$ is a fundamental system of
      roots in $Delta$, and that the associated Coxeter system in $W(Delta)$ is
      indecomposable, of type $B_n - C_n$.
    ] <case:type-b-fundamental-roots>],
  )
] <exc:hyperoctahedral-root-systems>
#idx("Hyperoctahedral group", target: [@exc:hyperoctahedral-root-systems])

#exercise[
  Let $Delta$ be a root system, and let $Delta_(+,1)$ and $Delta_(+,2)$ be two
  positive sets of roots in $Delta$. Prove that there exists $w in W(Delta)$
  such that $w Delta_(+,1)=Delta_(+,2)$.
  #hint([@exc:fundamental-root-systems-conjugate])[
    (Steinberg): Let $n=abs(Delta_(+,1) inter -Delta_(+,2))$. If $n=0$, then
    $Delta_(+,1)=Delta_(+,2)$. Let $n>0$. Then prove that
    $abs(Pi_1 inter -Delta_(+,2))>0$, where $Pi_1$ is the fundamental system of
    roots contained in $Delta_(+,1)$. Let $alpha in Pi_1 inter -Delta_(+,2)$,
    and show that $abs(s_alpha Delta_(+,1) inter -Delta_(+,2))=n-1$, so
    $abs(Delta_(+,1) inter -s_alpha Delta_(+,2))=n-1$ and an induction
    hypothesis can be applied.
  ]
] <exc:fundamental-root-systems-conjugate>

#exercise[
  Let $(W,S)$ be an indecomposable Coxeter system, with $W$ a finite group.
  Prove that the Coxeter graph associated with $(W,S)$ is a tree.
  #hint([@exc:finite-coxeter-graph-tree])[
    (Bourbaki @bib:Bourbaki1968): We have to prove that the Coxeter graph
    contains no closed paths of length $>1$. Suppose, to the contrary, that a
    closed path does exist. Let $V$ be the vector space affording the reflection
    representation, with basis ${e_1,...,e_n}$ and bilinear form $B$ (see
    @th:finite-coxeter-geometric-representation). By reindexing, we may assume
    that $e_1,...,e_m$ correspond to the ordered set of reflections which define
    a closed path in the Coxeter graph. Let $e=e_1+dots+e_m$. Then $e!=0$, and
    $B(e,e)=m+2sum_(i<j)B(e_i,e_j)$. Since
    $B(e_i,e_(i+1))= #source(592) -cos(pi/m_(i,i+1))<=-cos(pi/3)=-1/2$, for
    $1<=i<=m-1$ and similarly for $B(e_m,e_1)$, we obtain $B(e,e)<=0$,
    contradicting the fact that $B$ is positive definite (by
    @th:finite-coxeter-geometric-representation).
  ]
] <exc:finite-coxeter-graph-tree>

#exercise[
  Let $Delta$ be a root system of type $B_2$, consisting of the roots
  ${plus.minus e_i,plus.minus e_i plus.minus e_j:i!=j,1<=i,j<=2}$ in the
  notation of Exercise~@exc:hyperoctahedral-root-systems. Let $alpha_1=e_1-e_2$,
  $alpha_2=e_2$, $s_i=s_(alpha_i)$, $i=1,2$. Then ${s_1,s_2}$ is a set of
  fundamental reflections in $W(Delta)$. Verify that the subgroups
  $W_1=chevron.l s_1,s_2 s_1 s_2 chevron.r$ and
  $W_2=chevron.l s_2,s_1 s_2 s_1 chevron.r$ are both g.g.r.'s and that neither
  is a parabolic subgroup. Show that $W_1 inter W_2$ is not a g.g.r.
] <exc:reflection-subgroup-intersection-not-reflection>

#exercise[
  Let $Delta$ be a root system, and let $W$ denote the g.g.r. associated with
  $Delta$. Let $S$ be a set of fundamental reflections in $W$, defined by a set
  of positive roots $Delta_+ subset Delta$, and let $J subset.eq S$. Prove that
  $Delta_w^- subset.eq Delta_J$ for each $w in W_J$ (see
  §~@ss:finite-coxeter-parabolic-subgroups).
] <exc:parabolic-inversion-root-subsystem>
